<PageLayout meta={meta}>
  <h1 class="title mb-12">About</h1>
  <div class="prose prose-sm prose-cactus max-w-none">
    <p><b>Real human being, fake name.</b></p>
    <p>Abraham "Abe" Minus is a pseudonym. A sobriquet. It's not my real name. I choose to use a different name because I've used the internet before. I don't trust people to do the right thing in this space.</p>
    <p>I (the real me) am perfectly happy to fade into the background, with no one ever knowing my real name. I would not be surprised if people found out. If someone does go through all the trouble and succeeds, good for them. For those who need <i>something</i>: American, from Southern California, born during the Nixon administration.</p>
    <p>Sometimes I feel we are all living in the wrong timeline. I have trouble reconciling all the wonderful memories of my childhood with the present state of the world. I do hope that somehow, things get back on track. But I feel like my youth was an empire that has fallen, a civilization destroyed. It is a fading memory and I can never go back there. I cannot understand how others can just let it go or forget.</p>
    <p><b>Reference books and links to digital materials.</b></p>
    <p>Some people out there do crummy things like editing sources of information to suit their needs. How can I be sure that the definitions I am searching for are accurate? I use older printed books from before the web. My trusty printed reference guides that I have owned since high school are safe. There are exceptions, of course. Any copy of the Christian bible, for example. How many versions of that book do we need? What a mess.</p>
      <ul>
      <li><i>Webster's New World Dictionary of the American Language</i> (1980). A robust, collegiate-level dictionary. Despite being a heavy block of paper, it can be quicker than consulting the web. It is perfect for anyone who doesn’t care to read the top-of-page "AI" definitions you get from Google. Whoever thought that was a good idea must have been smoking something. Printed books don't hallucinate.</li>
      <li><i>Webster's Standard American Style Manual</i> (1986). A plain, but beautifully-executed style reference book. Perfect for those looking to get answers to many of the formatting questions about American English.</li>
      <li>From time to time, you will notice my references link to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Page" target="_blank"> Wikipedia</a> articles. Although they can be heavily edited, I have a naive belief that somewhere out there other editors trying to right the wrongs. Be sure to check the edit history if you are ever in doubt.</li>
      <li>For linking to books and other texts, <a href="https://archive.org" target="_blank"> Archive.org</a> is a place where thoughtful people have scanned and uploaded millions of pages of texts. You will have to trust me that I am linking to the correct version.</li>
      </ul>
    <p>This is a self-hosted website. All writing, documents, and servers are somewhere <i>not</i> in the US, in an apartment, in a room, on a desk. All work is my own. Make of that what you will.</p>
    <p>Clone or fork this <a class="cactus-link inline-block" href="https://github.com/chrismwilliams/astro-cactus" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">repo</a> if you like the theme...it isn't mine.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
